Fix 1, timer (supervisor.go:725, case <-backoff): new test seam Supervisor.backoffTaken (nil in production, :165), then the same non-blocking check k37 does at the exit. A waiting stop, start or restart goes through handle() instead of the timer's start: the stop cancels it and nothing spawns; a start or restart runs while backoff is still set, so it gets ReplacesRestart exactly as k153 defines it (crash evidence kept), and it is never Automatic. A waiting status is answered after the timer's start, as on the next turn. The delayed first start goes through the same path (a start waiting then is a plain start, not ReplacesRestart).

Fix 2, exit with a waiting start or restart (:797, :809): takenOver suppresses OnExit only. Everything else counts the exit as before. Choices, mine:
- lastExit: set to the exit, then cleared by the start or restart as StartService and RestartService always do. So the reply shows no lastExit, the same as for a call that comes right after the exit. I did not keep lastExit across the call: that would contradict the documented "ue_start and ue_restart remove it" (iterate SKILL.md:55, README:1129). If "recorded in lastExit" meant it should survive the call, that is a small change plus a doc amendment. Your call.
- k70 fast-fail: the exit is counted (countRun) and may trigger the give-up line; the call then resets quickFails and restarts as any manual start does (TestManualStartResetsTheFailCount covers the reset).
- k153/k128 crash episode: where the policy restarts the exit, the backoff is still armed and the call takes its place (ReplacesRestart). Where it does not (restart: never, maxRestarts used up, give-up), afterGiveUp is still set and the call's start says AfterGiveUp. Either way the dumps of the crash that just happened are kept, the same as when OnExit fired and the user then restarted.
- The launcher: OnExit not firing also means sess.agentExited is not called for that run; the agent is running again, so the exit code follows its next run.
Docs in the code updated: ServiceSpec.OnExit, Start.ReplacesRestart, the exit-branch comment.

